Title: The Glass Harbour
Credit: Written by
Author: Alex Rivera
Draft date: 08/28/2026

INT. THE GLASS HARBOUR DINER - LATE AFTERNOON #1#

ONE SHOT. NO CUTS. FIVE MINUTES, ONE BREATH.
A 1941 diner, re-topped in '52 and kept spotless — gleaming like the day it opened. Checkerboard tile, chrome stools, boomerang formica. On the plate glass, hand-painted and only just beginning to craze, a harbour scene and the name of the place, reading backwards from in here. Late gold light comes through it low and cuts the room in half.
We begin eight inches off the floor, gliding forward on a slow dolly. Past the kick-plates of the empty stools. Past a dropped sugar packet. Past scuffed work boots and, across the aisle, saddle shoes that have not moved in some time. The camera rises as it travels, comes up over the lip of a corner booth, and finds them.
RAY MERCER, fifty-seven, canvas work jacket he has owned too long, felt hat on the bench beside him, hands too big for his cup. He got here twenty minutes early.
Across from him, JUNE MERCER, twenty-three. Coat on. Bag still on her shoulder. Sitting on the edge of the bench the way you sit in a waiting room.
Between them: his coffee, a salt shaker, a pepper shaker. Nothing touched. She did not order anything.

RAY
You look like her.

JUNE
People say that.

Ray smiles. Not knowing how else to respond.
The camera eases toward Ray. Favoring him. Waiting with him. He moves uncomfortably in his seat.

RAY
Coffee's still bad here.

JUNE
Mom used to get the chowder. You'd order the coffee anyway and tell the waitress it was terrible, and she'd laugh like she hadn't heard it before.

RAY
Every other Sunday.

JUNE
Every other Sunday.

He almost smiles. He shouldn't.
The word lands on the table like a set of keys. Ray stops.

JUNE (CONT'D)
You called me at work.

RAY
I didn't have your other number.

JUNE
You had my home number for more than a year. It's the same one.

He turns his cup a quarter turn. The camera drifts with his hands and finds her over his shoulder.

JUNE (CONT'D)
So why now?

RAY
Do I need a why?

JUNE
It's been nine years.

She lets it sit. He has no answer, and the answer he doesn't have is louder than one.
Ray looks at the painted glass.

JUNE (CONT'D)
Is she in the car? Because I'm not doing that today.

RAY
Elaine left in March.

JUNE
The one you left us for?

Ray nods uncomfortably.
June laughs. It comes out wrong, and once it's out she can't put it back.

JUNE
March.

RAY
Sweetie —

JUNE
Don't sweetie me. Five months.

He doesn't answer. She looks out at the water, then back at him.

JUNE (CONT'D)
Five!

RAY
That isn't what this is.

JUNE
It's exactly what this is.

Ray takes a folded sheet of paper out of his inside pocket. Soft at the folds, gone yellow along one edge. He puts it down and pushes it two inches into the middle of the table.

RAY
Before you say the rest of it. Read this.

She looks at it. She does not touch it.

JUNE
No.

RAY
You don't have to say anything after. Just —

JUNE
No.

He leaves it out there a moment longer. Then he draws it back an inch toward himself and stops, and something goes out of him. He looks at his own hands.
The camera crosses the table low, over the folded page lying where he left it, and comes up on her side. We are with her now.

JUNE (CONT'D)
You sat me down in this booth and you bought me a strawberry milkshake and you told me here. Then you drove me home and by Thursday you were gone.

RAY
I know what I did.

JUNE
You took the Buick because it had the heater and you left mom the one that didn't start in the cold. And then it got cold.

RAY
That car was in my name.

JUNE
I found her on the kitchen floor. She wasn't crying — that's the part nobody — she was sitting with her back against the dishwasher and she wouldn't get up, and I was fourteen, and I made us eggs. It was the only thing I knew how to make.

Ray's jaw works. He says nothing. The camera holds on him, unkind about it.

JUNE (CONT'D)
Then a check every month. No note. Nine years of a man who could find the address but not a pen.

RAY
I sent notes.

JUNE
You didn't send notes.

RAY
Not all of them. I sent some.

JUNE
I would have kept them.

RAY
(too fast, too loud)
I was forty-eight and I was drowning in that house and nobody in it ever once asked me if I —

He hears it. Stops. The room hears it too.
The camera pushes in. Closer than either would allow if they noticed.

JUNE
Say the rest.

RAY
No.

JUNE
If anybody was drowning in that house — say the rest.

RAY
There isn't a rest that helps me.

JUNE
Try anyway.

Ray sets his hands flat on the table. It costs him.

RAY
I knew.

JUNE
What.

RAY
That's what I came to say and I've been sitting here forty minutes not saying it. I knew what I was doing. All that year. It wasn't a fog. I used to sit in the driveway with the engine off and count the windows with a light on. And then I'd go anyway.

Silence. A radio behind the counter goes somewhere sad and finishes.

RAY (CONT'D)
I chose it.

June has gone very still.

JUNE
That's worse.

RAY
I know.

JUNE
Do you understand that's worse? I've had nine years to build a man who didn't know what he was doing. I had him all ready. He was weak and he was sad and I could have forgiven him.

RAY
I'm not asking you to forgive anything.

JUNE
Then what am I supposed to do with you?

Her voice cracks and she's furious that it did. The camera stays close and does not look away.

JUNE (CONT'D)
I practiced this in the car.

RAY
What'd you practice.

JUNE
Something meaner.

RAY
You can still do it.

JUNE
I know I can.
(beat)
I don't want to.

Ray puts two fingers on the folded page, still lying where he left it an hour ago, and slides it the rest of the way across the formica.
The camera drops to the tabletop, follows it all the way over, and rises on her side.

JUNE (CONT'D)
What is it.

RAY
I wrote it when you were fifteen. Never had the guts to mail it. It's been in the glovebox of three cars.

JUNE
What does it say?

He doesn't answer. He looks at the page and not at her, and lets the question go by.
June looks at it. She does not touch it. Then she takes it and puts it in her bag without looking.

RAY (CONT'D)
There's a thing I want to ask and I know I don't have the standing to ask it.

JUNE
Then ask it badly.

RAY
Could we do this again. Not a whole thing. Not holidays. Coffee.

He puts his hand out into the middle of the table, palm up. Shaking slightly. It stays there far too long. The camera comes down to the formica and holds on that open hand, waiting the way he is waiting.
June doesn't take it. Instead she reaches out, takes his open hand in both of hers and folds the fingers closed. It is the only time in the scene that they touch.

JUNE
Not yet.

RAY
Okay.

Ray nods. Once. He can't say anything else, so he doesn't try. Something in his face lifts anyway, and he works to keep it off her.
June looks at the door. Then she lifts the bag off her shoulder, sets it on the bench beside her, and stays.
He doesn't get up.
The camera draws back off the table into a level two-shot across the booth and holds there. The low sun is still coming through the glass behind them, the harbour beyond it. He is trying not to show what he is feeling. She has not decided anything.
Two people in a corner booth. Not talking. Not leaving.
FADE OUT.